catalog.howItWorks:
BepInEx's flat `key=value` `.cfg` cannot express a list of networks each with a list of members, so put the topology in its own file and keep the `.cfg` for scalars. Poll `File.GetLastWriteTimeUtc` on a 5 s timer exactly as `WonderlandPlugin.PollConfigFile` does (Plugin.cs:97), parse into an immutable model, validate, and only then swap the live model (never mutate in place mid-tick).

**Members are resolved by position, never by ZDOID** — ZDOIDs are renumbered every world load (`ZDO.Load` :74552). Resolution: for each declared `at`, scan the census for the nearest portal ZDO within `radius` (default 6 m); ambiguity (two portals inside the radius) is a validation error, not a coin flip.

```jsonc
// BepInEx/config/TortalPortalLite.networks.json
{
  "schema": 1,
  "world": "Wubarrk",          // advisory; warn if != ZNet.instance.GetWorldName() (:80876)
  "defaults": { "reassertSeconds": 2.0, "onMissingMember": "warn", "tagCase": "exact" },
  "networks": [
    { "id": "merchant-ring", "enabled": true, "topology": "ring", "tag": "TRADE",
      "members": [
        { "name": "Haldor",   "at": [1240.5, 31.2, -3310.0], "radius": 6.0 },
        { "name": "Docks",    "at": [-210.0, 33.9, 180.0],   "radius": 6.0 },
        { "name": "Mistvale", "at": [4420.0, 95.4, 2180.0],  "radius": 6.0 }
      ] },
    { "id": "spawn-hub", "enabled": true, "topology": "hub", "tag": "HUB",
      "hub":   { "name": "Spawn Gate", "at": [0.0, 32.0, 4.0], "radius": 8.0 },
      "rotation": { "mode": "cycle", "dwellSeconds": 120 },
      "spokes": [
        { "name": "Swamp",  "at": [-980.0, 30.5, 1420.0] },
        { "name": "Plains", "at": [2600.0, 41.0, -700.0] }
      ] },
    { "id": "lore-gate", "enabled": false, "topology": "oneway-chain", "tag": "DEEP",
      "members": [ {"name":"A","at":[10,30,10]}, {"name":"B","at":[20,30,20]} ] }
  ]
}
```

**`tag` is load-bearing, not cosmetic.** `Game.ConnectPortals` phase 1 clears any connection whose partner's `s_tag` string differs (:100601) using bare ordinal `string !=`. Every member of a network must carry one byte-identical tag or vanilla tears the network down within 5 s. The validator must therefore reject two networks sharing a tag, and reject a tag that collides with an unmanaged portal's tag.

Validation before swap: unique `id`; unique `tag`; ≥2 members for ring/pairs; hub has ≥1 spoke; no member appears twice; no `at` beyond ±16320 m (`ZoneSystem.SectorToIndex` clamps out-of-range zones to sector 0, :115755-115774); no `at` with y>3000 unless explicitly allowed (`Character.InInterior` :4538). On any error, keep the previous model, log every error, and write them to the `.cmd.out` file.

`ZNetView.LoadFields()` (:82669, called from `ZNetView.Awake` :82631 and byte-identical in the client assembly at :82898) walks `GetComponentsInChildren<MonoBehaviour>()` on the freshly instantiated prefab and, for every PUBLIC INSTANCE FIELD, overrides its value from the ZDO. The keys are plain strings hashed by `ZDO.GetBool(string)`:
- `"HasFields"` — bool gate for the whole mechanism
- `"HasFieldsTeleportWorld"` — bool gate for that component
- `"TeleportWorld.m_allowAllItems"` — the payload

Supported types are int, float, bool, Vector3, string, GameObject (by prefab name) and ItemDrop (by prefab name).

`TeleportWorld` exposes exactly the right fields as public instance members: `m_allowAllItems` (bool, :143429), `m_exitDistance` (float, :143413), `m_activationRange` (float, :143411), `m_hoverOffset` (float, :143431). Server side:
```
zdo.SetOwner(ZDOMan.GetSessionID());
zdo.Set("HasFields", true);
zdo.Set("HasFieldsTeleportWorld", true);
zdo.Set("TeleportWorld.m_allowAllItems", true);
zdo.Set("TeleportWorld.m_exitDistance", 3.0f);
ZDOMan.instance.SetDirtyPortals();
ZDOMan.instance.ForceSendZDO(zdo.m_uid);
```
`m_allowAllItems` is consumed at `TeleportWorld.Teleport`'s fourth gate, `player.IsTeleportable(m_allowAllItems)` (:143533), which forwards to `Inventory.IsTeleportable(bool allowAllItems)` (:68860).

This directly refutes the "no per-portal knobs exist" conclusion that follows from reading the field list alone: the fields are not ZDO-backed, but the ZDO can nonetheless drive them, through vanilla's own reflection loader, on a 100% vanilla client.

What it is NOT: enforcement. It is client-honoured in exactly the sense vanilla's boss gating is — a modified client can ignore it. And it is generous-only in practice: you can turn ore transport ON for a portal a player paid for, but turning it OFF is already the default, so there is nothing to police.

Economic shape: an Ore Gate is a premium SKU. It costs a large one-off payment (or a trophy on the turnstile), it is attached to one specific portal pair, and it is the only way to move metal without a boat. That is the single most valuable thing a Valheim server can sell, and it is a one-field write.

`DamageText.Awake` registers the routed RPC `"RPC_DamageText"(ZPackage)` (:46352-46356). Payload is `{int TextType, Vector3 pos, string text, bool mySelf}`; `RPC_DamageText` localizes the string and hands it to `AddInworldText` (:46494-46510). Server side:

  var p = new ZPackage();
  p.Write((int)DamageText.TextType.Bonus);
  p.Write(portalPos + Vector3.up * 2.2f);
  p.Write("→ HALDOR");
  p.Write(false);
  ZRoutedRpc.instance.InvokeRoutedRPC(peer.m_uid, "RPC_DamageText", p);

`TextType` is `Normal, Resistant, Weak, Immune, Heal, TooHard, Blocked, Bonus` (:46309-46319). `Bonus` is the attention-grabber — orange, 1.5× font, ~3 s. AVOID `TooHard` and `Blocked`: those branches overwrite or prefix the string with `$msg_toohard` / `$msg_blocked:`, mangling whatever you sent. `Heal` is green and reads as positive confirmation; `Immune`/`Weak` have distinct colours usable as a status palette.

CULLING: `m_maxTextDistance = 30f` (:46338) plus `Hud.IsUserHidden()`. Beyond 30 m nothing renders — strictly a 'label the thing in front of you' channel.

WHY USE IT over a toast: it is anchored in 3D to a specific object. With three portals side by side, a toast saying `Dialed → Haldor` does not tell you WHICH portal changed; a Bonus text over the middle one does. On entering a hub, push one DamageText per portal in range naming its current destination and the whole hub is annotated in-world for three seconds without hovering anything.

Strictly per-peer — send only to the player who acted, so a hub does not spam text for everyone standing there.
